.TH _PRINTF 1 "March 2026" "1.0" "Programmer's Manual"
.SH NAME
_printf \- formatted output conversion and printing to standard output
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include "main.h"
.PP
.BI "int _printf(const char *" format ", ...);"
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B _printf()
function produces formatted output to the standard output
.IR stdout .
It works similarly to the
.BR printf (3)
function from the C standard library, but is a custom implementation.
.PP
The
.I format
string is a character string, possibly containing
.IR "conversion specifiers" ,
each introduced by the
.BR %
character, followed by a conversion character.
.SH CONVERSION SPECIFIERS
.TP
.B %c
Converts the next argument (of type
.IR int )
to an
.I unsigned char
and writes it to the standard output.
.TP
.B %s
Converts the next argument (of type
.IR "char *" )
to a character string. Characters are written up to the terminating null byte
.RB ( '\\0' ).
If the argument is
.BR NULL ,
the behavior is undefined.
.TP
.B %d
Converts the next argument (of type
.IR int )
to its signed decimal representation and writes it to the standard output.
.TP
.B %i
Identical to
.BR %d .
Converts the next argument (of type
.IR int )
to its signed decimal representation and writes it to the standard output.
.TP
.B %%
Writes a literal
.B %
character to the standard output. No argument is consumed.
.SH RETURN VALUE
On success,
.B _printf()
returns the number of characters written to the standard output (not including the terminating null character).
.PP
On error (
.I format
string is
.BR NULL ,
or invalid specifier), the function returns
.BR \-1 .
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Print a string:
.PP
.RS
.nf
_printf("Hello, %s!\n", "world");
.fi
.RE
.PP
Print an integer:
.PP
.RS
.nf
_printf("The value is: %d\n", 42);
.fi
.RE
.PP
Print a character and a percentage sign:
.PP
.RS
.nf
_printf("Character: %c, rate: 100%%\n", 'A');
.fi
.RE
.PP
Combine multiple specifiers:
.PP
.RS
.nf
int n;
n = _printf("Name: %s, Age: %i\n", "Alice", 30);
_printf("Number of characters written: %d\n", n);
.fi
.RE
.SH NOTES
This function is a pedagogical reimplementation of
.BR printf (3).
It does not handle width, precision options, or length modifiers.
Behaviors not described in this manual page are undefined.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR printf (3),
.BR write (2),
.BR stdarg (3),
.BR va_list (3)
.SH AUTHORS
Written as part of the
.B _printf
project at
